# The presence of this file is the whole message: s6-supervise starts a service
# whose directory contains a `down` file in the DOWN state. s6 never reads the
# contents, so this text is here purely for whoever finds the file on a printer
# and wants to know why it exists.
#
# WHY THE CAMERA SHIPS DOWN. The moment s6-svscan notices this directory it
# starts an s6-supervise for it, and without this file that supervise would
# launch mjpg-streamer immediately -- before payload/init.d/S65camera has
# looked for the video device, checked that FlashForge's streamer binary is
# still on the firmware partition where it expects it, or read the resolution
# and port out of anvil.conf. Started that early it would fail on a printer
# with no camera, be restarted, and fail again, filling the boot log with the
# one message that is guaranteed not to be the actual problem.
#
# This used to be argued from a switch, MOD_CAM, which no longer exists. The
# argument survives it unchanged, because it was never about the switch: it is
# that the SCANNER cannot make this decision and S65camera can. With this file
# the service sits down and idle, costing one ~30KB supervisor and nothing
# else, until S65camera brings it up with `s6-svc -u`.
#
# The state is not sticky across boots, and that is correct: `down` is consulted
# by s6-supervise when the scandir is scanned, which happens fresh on every
# boot, so a camera brought up by hand for a test does not silently outlive the
# session it was brought up in.
